<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Statute of Rhuddlan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1284">1284</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Statute of Wales</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Statute of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhuddlan">Rhuddlan</a>, issued by Edward I at Rhuddlan Castle in March 1284, set out the government of the lands of Gwynedd and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/principality-of-wales">Principality of Wales</a> after the conquest of 1282–1283. It created shires such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglesey">Anglesey</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caernarfon">Caernarfon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merionethshire">Merioneth</a> and Flint, introduced English criminal law while keeping some Welsh civil custom, and remained the basis of royal government there until the Laws in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> Acts of 1535–1542.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/statute-of-rhuddlan.jpg" alt="Statute of Rhuddlan" width="320" height="444" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statute_of_Rhuddlan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
